WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.710
A good game show is a well-oiled, million-dollar
machine, built to ensure both fun and fairness.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.970
But with tons of cash on the line, some contestants
just can't resist the urge to find a way to

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.059
beat or cheat the system and win big.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:16.260
Here are a few of the biggest game show cheaters
in history… or at least the ones who were

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:17.539
caught.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:18.840
Charles Ingram

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.260
Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:25.560
really wants contestants to win, since it's
just good TV to see a regular Joe get rich.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.340
Even with multiple lifelines available to
contestants for especially difficult trivia

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.090
questions, that just wasn't enough for disgraced
Army Major Charles Ingram, who appeared on

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.510
the British edition of the show in 2001.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:41.219
According to Vice, Ingram stationed two people
in the audience: his wife, Diana, and his

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:43.969
friend, Tecwen Whittock, both former contestants.

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:47.959
As he carefully read off each of the four
multiple choice answers out loud, he'd listen

00:00:47.959 --> 00:00:52.129
for a cough, which was allegedly a signal
from his plants as to which answer was the

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:53.319
correct one.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:57.309
Using this ridiculous method pioneered by
cheating high school students, Ingram actually

00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:59.649
won the million-pound grand prize.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:03.549
The scheme was ultimately discovered and all
three were found guilty of, quote, "procuring

00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:08.810
the execution of a valuable security by deception,"
or, in simpler terms: fraud.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.729
All three received suspended prison sentences,
and were fined a combined total of 55,000

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:17.020
pounds… which Charles allegedly never paid.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.619
Ingram was also stripped of his title by the
Army Board, after 17 years of service.

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:25.409
As if that weren't enough bad karma, Charles
Ingram later slipped on an apple while mowing

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:28.460
his lawn and cut off three of his toes.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.560
Sorry, Charlie.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.670
Adriana Abenia

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:38.539
In 2014, Spanish model Adriana Abenia appeared
on Pasapalabra, Spain's version of Password.

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:42.320
Abenia's blatant cheating scheme occurred
during a segment where she had to listen to

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.530
song clips and identify the name and performer.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.960
The game had barely started when the host
and other contestants noticed that she kept

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.140
looking at a phone she had hidden in her lap.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.229
She'd been using the music identification
app Shazam.

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:57.130
She was called out on it right on the show,
but everyone just laughed it off.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.130
The host, Christian Galvez, even said that
she deserved a special prize for cheating

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:02.130
so brazenly.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.250
A few days later, Abenia made her intentions
clear, saying,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.390
"No one told me that [I] could not cheat."

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.289
"You also have to understand that it was to
help someone else.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.310
I'm very altruistic."

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.230
Khaled El-Katateny

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:17.700
Millionaire Hot Seat is an Australian spinoff
of Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?, and in

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.990
2014, a cocky 19-year-old law student named
Khaled El-Katateny appeared on the show and

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.920
racked up winnings of $100,000.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.560
"Where's the cellophane?"

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.180
"Where's the cellophane?

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:28.680
I want the cellophane."

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.680
"What was that?"

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.940
"I want the cellophane."

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.320
"Nah, you only get that for $250,000."

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.360
After the show aired, he told the media that
he cheated — sort of, saying,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.290
"I didn't play the game — I played the man."

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:41.290
Huh?

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.120
According to El-Katateny, he claimed to nail
correct answers to the questions not through

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.870
knowledge, but by watching the body language
of host Eddie McGuire as he read off the multiple

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.440
choice possibilities, admitting,

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.440
"If you look at it, you see me working Eddie
McGuire.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.890
I'm reading every single subtle thing about
his face."

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.920
He also watched the studio audience behind
McGuire to see which choice made their faces

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.030
light up.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:05.860
In the end, El-Katateny got to keep his money,
because utilizing a poker strategy for a trivia

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.950
game technically isn't against the rules.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.459
When asked about his plans for his "winnings,"
El-Katateny simply said, quote, "I didn't

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:16.370
win anything — I earned it."

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:17.680
Terry Kniess

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.430
The Price is Right seems like a difficult
game show to win because it involves guessing

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.439
the, quote, "actual retail price" of items
— which never seems to be anywhere near

00:03:25.439 --> 00:03:27.690
what you'd see at an actual retail store.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:35.170
So, when Terry Kniess put in a bid of $23,743
during a "Showcase Showdown" in 2008 — and

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.319
hit the prize's retail price exactly — it
defied all of the odds.

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:40.319
How'd he do it?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.080
"Hasn't happened since '72 or '73.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.319
Hit it right on the nose."

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:48.420
Esquire's profile of Kniess revealed his secrets:
the man is an analytical genius.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.621
Kneiss has been both an expert blackjack player
and an award-winningly accurate meteorologist.

00:03:52.621 --> 00:03:56.700
"I'm Terry Kniess, you might be better off
sitting at home and roasting your nuts on

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.260
an open fire than going out on the road tomorrow."

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:03.849
Kniess told Esquire that he and his wife recorded
episodes of The Price is Right every day for

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:08.920
four months, then memorized the prices of
all the items the show used, and frequently

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.049
reused, in the Showcase Showdown segment.

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:13.079
From there, it was just basic math.

00:04:13.079 --> 00:04:14.730
It wasn't even a new strategy.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.920
"How did you you settle on $21.40."

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.400
"I seen that on here last week and that's
what it is.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:23.920
I watch you every day!"

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:28.460
Host Drew Carey floated another theory: in
the audience during the taping was Ted Slauson,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.710
a regular Price attendee and one-time contestant,
who had also amassed an encyclopedic memory

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.510
of showcase prices.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:39.850
Carey and show producers seemed to think that
Slauson colluded with Kniess and used hand

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:43.390
signals to tell him the perfect price, an
allegation that both men deny.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:47.540
Kniess got to keep his winnings, because despite
their suspicions, the show couldn't prove

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.140
he did anything wrong...other than being real
good at math.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.240
"Get those nerds!"

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.280
"Nerds!"

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.320
"NERDSSS!"

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:55.860
Korilla BBQ

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.640
In 2011, the NYC-based food truck, Korilla
BBQ, was eliminated from The Great Food Truck

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:06.060
Race, a reality show competition in which
hopeful street chefs compete for a grand prize

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.760
of $50,000.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.000
The team with the lowest sales gets eliminated,
hence Korilla's desperate, if not ill-conceived

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.220
attempt at staying alive with some bogus profit.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.530
"Unfortunately, one of you guys tried to cheat."

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:25.700
"It was clear you put $2,700 of your own money
in the till."

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:30.380
The owners of the Korean-Mexican fusion truck
maintained their innocence, despite a confidentiality

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.990
agreement that barred them from speaking out
in too much detail.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.760
Food Network originally only said that the
team was eliminated from the show after making,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.780
quote, "an unfortunate decision."

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.830
It wasn't until a 2016 interview with KoreanAmericanStory.org
that Korilla BBQ owner, Eddie Song, finally

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.500
cleared up what really happened.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:51.150
Apparently, the team decided to get creative
when facing the challenge of not being able

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.750
to sell meat while competing in Memphis.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:57.520
Song and his crew, quote, "formed a little
partnership with one of the top Memphis-style

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.130
BBQ" joints, and ended up selling empty tortillas...
and sending their customers elsewhere to get

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.300
free meat.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.430
Ultimately, they bent the rules and got busted.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.100
The Twenty One gang

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.050
While most game show cheats are simply over-eager
rogue contestants, the scandal surrounding

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:16.990
the 1956 game show Twenty One involved the
show's producers — and served as the basis

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:19.660
for the 1994 movie, Quiz Show.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:23.930
According to Charles Van Doren's New Yorker
account of the scandal, producers Al Freedman,

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:28.270
Jack Barry, and Dan Enright colluded with
contestants Herb Stempel and Charles Van Doren

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:31.860
in some carefully choreographed high-stakes
game show drama.

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.820
After around six weeks of coached-winning
for Stempel, the show saw a dip in ratings,

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.740
possibly due to the unlikable nature of the
quirky New York postal clerk.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:44.750
Enter Charles Van Doren, a handsome English
professor from Columbia University, and son

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.110
of a prominent poet.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:51.010
He was similarly coached, and after some staged
episodes that ended in ties, Van Doren eventually

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.340
overtook Stempel.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.640
Both contestants walked away with a bunch
of cash, and ratings were up.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.120
Win-win, right?

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.120
Wrong.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.870
No one believed Stempel's claims about the
plague of game show fixing until a notebook

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:06.720
full of future quiz answers was found on the
set of a completely different game show.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.150
Stempel testified to a grand jury, which eventually
led to congressional hearings, and an amendment

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:16.320
to the Communications Act of 1934, making
it officially illegal to fix quiz shows.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.420
It didn't much matter, because the networks
were so spooked that they abruptly cancelled

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.900
many of their primetime game shows anyway.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.720
"We're looking for nuts, now.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.040
We're looking for nuts.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:27.600
NUTS!"

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:33.140
Thanks for watching!

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:35.530
